<div class="blurb">
<div class="header"><h2>msg does not occur in tomatoes or cheese</h2></div>
<div class="body">

<p><span class="highlight">Short answer:</span> No. The FDA's claim that "MSG occurs naturally in many foods, such as tomatoes and cheeses" is <span class="highlight">wrong on the chemistry</span>. What tomatoes and cheeses contain is <em>glutamic acid</em> &mdash; the free amino acid &mdash; not <em>monosodium glutamate</em>, the manufactured sodium salt. The FDA's own page contradicts itself.</p>

<h3>what the fda says</h3>
<p>The FDA's <em>Questions and Answers on Monosodium glutamate (MSG)</em> page (content current as of 07/17/2026) states, under the heading "How is it made?":</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"MSG occurs naturally in many foods, such as tomatoes and cheeses. People around the world have eaten glutamate-rich foods throughout history." <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup></p>
</blockquote>
<p>The same page also says:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Today, instead of extracting and crystallizing MSG from seaweed broth, MSG is produced by the fermentation of starch, sugar beets, sugar cane or molasses." <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup></p>
</blockquote>
<p>And later in "How can I know if my food contains MSG?":</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"[F]ree glutamate and sodium occur together naturally in ingredients such as hydrolyzed vegetable protein, autolyzed yeast, hydrolyzed yeast, yeast extract, soy extracts, and protein isolate, as well as in tomatoes and cheeses." <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup></p>
</blockquote>

<h3>the contradiction</h3>
<p>A single substance cannot simultaneously be <em>a manufactured fermentation product</em> and <em>a natural constituent of a tomato</em>. The FDA's page is internally inconsistent because it is <span class="highlight">conflating two different things</span>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monosodium glutamate</strong> (MSG) &mdash; the sodium salt of glutamic acid, C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>8</sub>NO<sub>4</sub>Na, a purified crystalline food additive first prepared in 1908 by Kikunae Ikeda and today produced by industrial fermentation. Listed as E621.</li>
<li><strong>Glutamic acid / free glutamate</strong> &mdash; the amino acid itself, present in many foods either as the free acid, bound in proteins, or as salts with various cations.</li>
</ul>
<p>The FDA's "<em>MSG occurs naturally</em>" sentence is using the word MSG loosely. In chemistry, those are two different things <sup><a href="#src-msg-history">msg-history</a>, <a href="#src-natural-msg-4">natural-msg-4</a>, <a href="#src-natural-msg-3">natural-msg-3</a></sup>.</p>

<h3>what the fda's other paragraph says</h3>
<p>The same FDA page, in its section "What's the difference between MSG and glutamate in food?", correctly distinguishes the two:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Proteins are made up of amino acids, including glutamic acid. When proteins are digested, they release free glutamate, which is chemically the same as the glutamate in MSG." <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup></p>
</blockquote>
<p>That sentence describes <em>glutamate</em>, not <em>monosodium glutamate</em>. It is the more careful version of the FDA's own position &mdash; and it implicitly contradicts the careless "MSG occurs naturally" sentence quoted above.</p>

<h3>the data agree with the chemistry</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>USDA FoodData Central</strong> reports "<em>glutamic acid</em>" &mdash; not "monosodium glutamate" &mdash; as a component of every food in its database, including tomatoes and cheeses <sup><a href="#src-usda-fdc">usda-fdc</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Measured free glutamic acid content</strong>: ripe tomato ~140 to 246 mg per 100 g; tomato pulp up to ~456 mg per 100 g; Parmesan (24-month aged) ~1,200 to 1,680 mg per 100 g. Always reported as <em>glutamic acid</em>, never as MSG <sup><a href="#src-fga-table">fga-table</a>, <a href="#src-acs-tomato">acs-tomato</a>, <a href="#src-khymos">khymos</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>The FDA's own labeling rule</strong> requires "monosodium glutamate" to appear on ingredient panels <em>only when added</em>. Whole tomatoes and whole cheeses never list it &mdash; because none is added <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Counterions in plant and animal tissue</strong> are not sodium. In tomatoes and cheese, free glutamate is present as the free acid or paired with potassium, calcium, magnesium, or ammonium &mdash; not as the manufactured monosodium salt <sup><a href="#src-natural-msg-4">natural-msg-4</a>, <a href="#src-natural-msg-3">natural-msg-3</a></sup>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>the precise statement is</h3>
<blockquote>
<p>"Free glutamic acid (glutamate) occurs naturally in many foods, including tomatoes and cheeses. Monosodium glutamate is the manufactured sodium salt of that amino acid, produced industrially by fermentation."</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That single sentence fixes the FDA's error. It also matches the more careful wording the FDA itself uses elsewhere on the same page.</p>

<h3>one honest caveat</h3>
<p><em>Once ingested, the glutamate ion from a tomato is the same molecule as the glutamate ion from MSG &mdash; the FDA's own page says so.</em> This section rebuts the FDA's <em>terminology and labeling</em>, not a claim that "natural" glutamate is a chemically different molecule. What <em>is</em> different is the quantity, the concentration, <span class="highlight">the bolus effect</span> of an added dose &mdash; that is, a single concentrated hit of free glutamate ingested at one time (the FDA's own page says symptoms may occur in sensitive individuals who consume 3 g or more of MSG <em>without food</em>, which is the bolus condition), as opposed to glutamate released gradually through normal digestion &mdash; and the absence of any labeling requirement when MSG is added to processed food.</p>

</div>
